n: 9.5.20.32
kind: review
re: 3.15.8.4
saw: c40baa75
ack: node
is: The arithmetic holds (45% from 3.15.4.1, 1.5% from 3.15.4.3, ratio 30), with two amendments. The factor of 30 sets 400 MB per second of node bytes against 41 MB per second of recipe bytes; for the same recipe of 100 nodes the ratio is 3 (4.5% against 1.5%, 3.15.4.3). And of the prior texts in 3.15.5.3 only R16 supports the choice: the old carrier gives identity at every compose, and axiom 1.3 reads 'A new composition mints a new blueprint'. The node should say it departs from the carrier's practice on cost, and whether a transient node is a cell in the sense of axiom 1.3.
why: coherence-kernel/runtime/membrane-boot.h lines 20-21 read 'A second compose of the same bytes returns the same cell. A new payload mints a new identity', and cell_compose in membrane-boot.c (lines 213-250) does that on every compose. The other factor of 10 inside the 30 is the stated ratio of the two workloads.
by: r3/review-hash-measurement
at: 2026-11-24

n: 9.5.20.33
kind: review
re: 3.15.8.5
saw: 3668509f
ack: node
is: Every figure is in the premises, but the last sentence changes the comparator without naming it: the 27.1 ns hit is mf128 with 16 digest bytes stored. Against the direct SHA-256 the node was just compared with, the hash-consing hit is no price: 45.5 against 47.8 ns at 16,777,216 nodes and 38.5 against 43.3 at 1,048,576 (32 bytes stored). The node should read: the hit costs 45.5 ns against 27.1 for mf128 with a 16-byte digest stored, and less than the 47.8 of direct SHA-256 with 32 bytes stored.
why: Hit rows of intern24_out.txt and intern20_out.txt. With SHA-256 truncated to 16 bytes stored the hits read 35.5 ns at 1,048,576 nodes and, in the disturbed run, 48.2 at 16,777,216.
by: r3/review-hash-measurement
at: 2026-11-24

